| Concept     | What it does                                                            | Where it lives     |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `services/` | Holds **pure logic** (classes, singletons, SDK interfaces, data layers) | `services/` folder |
| `hooks/`    | Holds **stateful logic** & **UI interactions** (React-specific)         | `hooks/` folder    |
| `context/`  | Provides state from hooks to components tree                            | `providers/`       |

## Mental Model for Hooks vs Services

- ✅ **Services** = Stateless, environment-agnostic, async-capable logic (can be reused in CLI, tests, or web)
- ✅ **Hooks** = Stateful logic, side-effects, user-triggered flows
- ❌ Don't put database access or class instances inside hooks
- ❌ Don’t call hooks in services (violates rules of hooks)

## Best Practices

- Keep services and hooks separate: services for pure logic/data, hooks for React state/effects.
- Providers (context) should wrap the app and supply state from hooks to the component tree.
- Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) for each folder to simplify imports.
- Shared code goes in `shared/` (components, hooks, utils, styles, types).
- Feature code is colocated in `features/` by domain (screens, hooks, components, types, etc.).

| components/ | Shared dumb UI components |
| hooks/ | Custom React hooks used app-wide |
| styles/ | Global + atomic CSS |
| types/ | Centralized types for reuse and clarity |

the services related to messaging will use [tests files from the Smash-Node-Lib (SmashMessaging) library](mdc:smash-node-lib/tests) as guidance on how to implement messaging functionalities.
